{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And Avraham took, etc.</b> Since Yishmael was a grown lad of thirteen years, it was therefore necessary to take him with words, to placate him. And likewise the ones born in his household and acquired with his money, for among them were adults, and he took them with persuasion, for he wanted all of them to accept upon themselves willingly the commandment of the King." "<b>as He spoke, etc.</b> We need to know why it was necessary to say ״as He spoke,״ etc. — I see that this is what he did. And perhaps it intends to say that that act of taking them with their consent was ״as Elohim spoke with him.״" "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to say that he did not delay from performing the commandment of HaShem but immediately and instantly — <b>as He spoke</b> means close together, and this is to inform of his alacrity, upon him be peace, in the service of the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend to say, after the manner of the Sages, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 27a): ״הַמּוֹל יִמּוֹל״ — that the circumciser must himself be circumcised. And this is what it says <b>as He spoke with him</b>, meaning that he preceded and circumcised himself first, and afterward circumcised the others. And according to this it is not difficult why the verse did not mention Avraham's circumcision, for two reasons he had to precede himself: both from the side of alacrity, ״correct yourself״ etc., and from the side of ensuring that they be circumcised according to the law that HaShem commanded him, ״הַמּוֹל יִמּוֹל.״" "And what the verse says afterward: ״וְאַבְרָהָם בֶּן תִּשְׁעִים וָתֵשַׁע שָׁנָה״ etc. (Genesis 17:24) — this is to inform of the time in which he was circumcised, just as it said ״וְיִשְׁמָעֵאל בֶּן שְׁלֹשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה,״ even so it mentioned it earlier. And according to what I explained it is not difficult, for the verse made known to us that he circumcised himself first, as written ״כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר אִתּוֹ״ etc., in the manner that I explained." "<b>Thus concludes the section of Lech Lecha.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {3 "The phrase קְשׁוֹט עַצְמְךָ ('correct yourself') is an abbreviated quotation of the Talmudic dictum ״קשוט עצמך ואחר כך קשוט אחרים״ ('correct yourself and afterward correct others'); I have rendered it literally with 'etc.' marking the truncation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:53.647018577Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}